# Azure Snowflake IoT Pipeline

## Why I Built This

I've spent nearly three years working as a data engineer in automotive manufacturing — building ETL pipelines, designing Kimball star schemas on SQL Server, and delivering Power BI dashboards from shop floor data. Everything I've built has been on-premises.

But I knew the next step in my career required cloud experience. Not just theory — actual hands-on work with real cloud services, real data, and real architecture decisions. So I set out to build what I would build if my company asked me to migrate our data platform to the cloud tomorrow.

This project is that answer.

---

## What This Project Does

This is an end-to-end cloud data pipeline that takes raw IoT sensor data and moves it through a full Medallion architecture (Bronze → Silver → Gold) on Azure and Snowflake, with two separate ingestion paths:

- **Batch Path** — CSV files ingested via Azure Data Factory on a schedule
- **Stream Path** — JSON events ingested via Snowpipe in near-real-time

Both paths land in Snowflake, get cleaned and transformed through Dynamic Tables, and serve analytics-ready data to Power BI dashboards and a Streamlit web application.

![Architecture Diagram](images/architecture.png)

---

## The Dataset

I used the IoT Environmental Sensor Telemetry dataset from Kaggle — roughly 405,000 rows of sensor readings including temperature, humidity, CO, LPG, smoke, light, and motion data.

I chose this because it mirrors what I work with in manufacturing — high-frequency machine sensor data that needs to be ingested reliably, cleaned, and made available for monitoring and analysis. The only difference is the source — instead of CMM machines and PLCs on a factory floor, it's IoT sensors. The engineering principles are the same.

---

## Architecture Decisions

### Why Medallion?

In my on-prem work, I already structure data in layers — raw ingestion tables, cleaned staging, and final star schema views for reporting. Medallion is the cloud-native version of the same thinking:

- **Bronze** — Raw data lands in ADLS Gen2, untouched. This is the single source of truth. If anything breaks downstream, I can always reprocess from here.
- **Silver** — Data moves into Snowflake, gets validated, deduplicated, and typed correctly. This is where bad records get flagged and handled.
- **Gold** — Views built on top of Silver that serve specific analytics needs — aggregated, filtered, and optimized for Power BI and Streamlit consumption.

### Why Two Ingestion Paths?

I wanted to prove I could handle both patterns because real manufacturing environments need both:

- **Batch (ADF → CSV)** — This is how most production reporting works today. End-of-shift dumps, daily quality summaries, weekly OEE reports. ADF handles this with scheduled triggers and pipeline monitoring.
- **Stream (Snowpipe → JSON)** — This is where manufacturing is heading. Real-time alerts when a sensor drifts out of spec, live dashboards showing machine status. Snowpipe auto-ingests JSON files as soon as they land in ADLS Gen2 — no scheduling, no polling.

In a production environment, I would replace the ADLS Gen2 landing zone with Azure IoT Hub or Event Hubs feeding through a Kafka connector into Snowpipe Streaming. This project uses the file-based approach because it demonstrates the same pipeline design without requiring live IoT hardware.

![Medallion Layers](images/medallion.png)

---

## Pipeline Walkthrough

### Batch Path
ADF runs on a scheduled trigger. It picks up CSV files from ADLS Gen2, loads them into Snowflake through a COPY INTO command, and logs the pipeline run. If a file fails validation, ADF catches it and routes it to an error container for investigation.

### Stream Path
Snowpipe watches the ADLS Gen2 container using event notifications. When a new JSON file lands, Snowpipe picks it up within seconds, parses it using a defined file format, and loads it into the Bronze table. Dynamic Tables handle the Silver transformation incrementally — only processing new rows, not rescanning the full table.

![Batch Pipeline](images/batch_pipeline.png)

![Stream Pipeline](images/stream_pipeline.png)

---

## Snowflake Design

| Object | Purpose |
|---|---|
| External Stage | Points to ADLS Gen2 container using SAS token |
| File Format | Defined CSV and JSON formats for consistent parsing |
| Snowpipe | Auto-ingest pipe triggered by Azure Event Grid notifications |
| Dynamic Tables | Incremental Silver layer — cleansing, typing, deduplication |
| Views | Gold layer — aggregated and filtered for specific reporting needs |

### Why Dynamic Tables Instead of dbt?

Dynamic Tables are Snowflake-native. They handle incremental transformation automatically without needing an external orchestrator. For a pipeline this size, adding dbt would be overengineering. Dynamic Tables let me define the transformation as a SQL query and Snowflake handles when and how to refresh — similar to a materialized view but with dependency tracking.

If this were a larger enterprise pipeline with 50+ models, I would consider dbt. For this use case, Dynamic Tables are the right tool.

---

## Security Design

Security was not an afterthought — I designed it from the start because in manufacturing, data access control is non-negotiable.

- **RBAC** — Separate Snowflake roles for ingestion (write-only), transformation (read-write on staging), and reporting (read-only on Gold). No single role has access to everything.
- **Key Pair Authentication** — ADF and Snowpipe connect to Snowflake using RSA key pairs, not passwords. This eliminates credential leakage risk and is the recommended approach for service-to-service connections.
- **SAS Tokens** — Scoped, time-limited access tokens for ADLS Gen2. The Snowflake external stage uses a SAS token with read-only access to only the specific container it needs — not the entire storage account.
- **Encryption** — Data encrypted at rest in both ADLS Gen2 (Azure-managed keys) and Snowflake (AES-256). All data in transit uses TLS.

---

## Reporting Layer

### Power BI

Connected to Snowflake Gold layer views using Snowflake's Power BI connector. Dashboards show sensor trends, threshold alerts, and summary statistics.

![Power BI Dashboard](images/powerbi.png)

### Streamlit

A lightweight Python web app connected directly to Snowflake using the Snowflake Python connector. Built for real-time monitoring — shows live sensor readings, trend charts, and anomaly flags.

![Streamlit App](images/streamlit.png)

---

## What I Would Do Differently in Production

This project was built as a proof of concept on a public dataset. In a real manufacturing deployment, I would change the following:

| This Project | Production Version |
|---|---|
| Kaggle CSV/JSON files | Azure IoT Hub or Event Hubs for live sensor data |
| File-based Snowpipe | Snowpipe Streaming via Kafka connector |
| Single Snowflake database | Separate databases per environment (dev/staging/prod) |
| Manual deployment | Infrastructure as code (Terraform or Azure Resource Manager) |
| SAS token for stage | Managed Identity for Azure-to-Snowflake trust |
| Power BI + Streamlit | Grafana for real-time monitoring, Power BI for management reporting |

---

## Key Learnings

**Medallion is not just a folder structure.** Each layer has a specific contract — Bronze is append-only raw data, Silver is validated and typed, Gold is business-logic applied. Skipping Silver or mixing concerns between layers creates the same mess you get with unstructured on-prem databases.

**Snowpipe is not true real-time.** It is near-real-time micro-batch — typically 1-2 minute latency. For sub-second requirements, Snowpipe Streaming or Kafka is needed. For most manufacturing monitoring use cases, 1-2 minutes is acceptable.

**Dynamic Tables simplify incremental processing.** Instead of writing custom merge logic or maintaining dbt models, Dynamic Tables handle change tracking internally. The tradeoff is less flexibility — but for straightforward cleansing and aggregation, they are excellent.

**Security should be designed first, not added later.** Setting up RBAC, key pair auth, and scoped tokens from the beginning is far easier than retrofitting them after the pipeline is already running with admin credentials.

